      {
        "field_id": "question_69093092",
        "label": "Why are you interested in this opportunity?",
        "type": "textarea",
        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:question_69093092",
        "action": "fill",
        "value": "I build systems where the interesting question is not how much a machine can do, but where its authority should stop, which is most of what security work is about. The clearest example is a Playwright-based job-application agent I built in TypeScript and Node. Fully autonomous browser agents are dangerous in that workflow because one wrong action submits a real application or fills a legal field incorrectly, so I put every mutating capability behind fail-closed environment flags that never default to enabled, used SQLite as the single source of truth so application state could not diverge across processes, and architecturally prevented free-text and demographic fields from being auto-filled. I also built a validation ladder from unit-confirmed up to live-mutation-confirmed, because a capability passing against a local fixture is not evidence it is safe against production. NISC runs software that 1,200+ energy cooperatives and communication organizations depend on, which is exactly the kind of environment where failure modes matter more than demos. I would want to work on the unglamorous parts: how access is gated, how issues get triaged and prioritized, and how an alert becomes something an analyst can actually act on rather than one more thing to ignore.",
        "reason": "Essay generated from the operator's about-me context"
      },
